# Several windows on the desktop: `terminal` opens one more terminal with a
# shell of its own. The window on top has the focus: a blue title bar
# (3a6ea5; the others are gray, 8a9bb0) and the keyboard.
@usb kbd mouse
$ layout us
$ terminal
a new terminal window (1 open): Alt+Tab or a click changes window, 'exit' or its close box ends it
# The new window is at 32,32 on top of the desktop; the console's window
# (from 70,74) lost the focus.
@screenshot 1920x1080 pixels 300,36=3a6ea5 300,100=000000 1500,78=8a9bb0 0,0=a8d0f0
$ threads
~ terminal   waiting
~ desktop    waiting

# The keyboard now types in the new window; its shell writes a file.
@keys e c h o spc h i spc shift+dot spc slash p a n e dot t x t ret
$ sleep 1
$ cat /pane.txt
hi

# Alt+Tab: the console's window comes to the top and gets the keys.
@keys alt+tab
@screenshot 1920x1080 pixels 1500,78=3a6ea5 300,100=000000
@keys e c h o spc b a c k ret
$wait
back

# A click on the other window brings it back to the top. The pointer
# starts in the middle of the screen (960,540); 20,20 is desktop only.
@mouse -940 -520
@mouse 30 20
@button left down
@button left up
@screenshot 1920x1080 pixels 300,36=3a6ea5 1500,78=8a9bb0

# `exit` typed there ends its shell, and the window goes away.
@keys e x i t ret
@screenshot 1920x1080 pixels 300,36=a8d0f0 1500,78=3a6ea5 0,0=a8d0f0
$ threads
!~ terminal
$ echo console again
console again

# A click on a window's close box (the pointer is at 50,40; the box of a
# new window at 32,32 is around 668,46) ends its shell and takes it away.
$ terminal
@screenshot 1920x1080 pixels 300,36=3a6ea5 668,46=e06c5a
@mouse 618 6
@screenshot 1920x1080 pixels 300,36=3a6ea5 668,46=000000 672,44=e06c5a
@button left down
@button left up
@screenshot 1920x1080 pixels 300,36=a8d0f0 1500,78=3a6ea5
$ threads
!~ terminal
# The console's own window has a close box too, but stays.
$ sleep
usage: sleep SECONDS   (0 to 3600)
$ sleep 0

# At most four, and none without the desktop.
$ terminal
$ terminal
$ terminal
$ terminal
$ terminal
terminal: too many terminal windows
$ desktop off
$ threads
!~ terminal
$ terminal
terminal: windows need the desktop (see desktop)
$ terminal now
usage: terminal
